<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sindh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sindh is a province of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> on the lower course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus River</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>. It was a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>, whose city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohenjo-daro">Mohenjo-daro</a> lies there. Arab forces under Muhammad ibn al-Qasim conquered it in 711 and 712, and later it was ruled by local dynasties, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, the Talpurs and, from 1843, the British.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sindh.jpg" alt="Sindh" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M.irfan44 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jinnah_Mausoleum.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In about 739 the Chalukya governor in Gujarat, Avanijanashraya Pulakeshin, repulsed an Arab raid from <b>Sindh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1749 the Mughal Emperor Ahmad Shah Bahadur ceded <b>Sindh</b>, the Punjab and the trans-Indus lands to save his capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 720s and 730s Arab governors of <b>Sindh</b>, notably Junayd, raided deep into western India, and the Gwalior inscription praises Nagabhata I for destroying the armies of a powerful foreign king, a claim usually linked to the repulse of these attacks around 738.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The name Pakistan was coined in January 1933 by the activist Choudhry Rahmat Ali in a pamphlet, Now or Never, as an acronym of Panjab, Afghania, Kashmir, <b>Sindh</b> and Baluchistan; in Persian and Urdu it also means 'the land of the pure'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Early Harappan phase, from about 3300 BCE, regional traditions named after sites such as Amri in <b>Sindh</b>, Kot Diji and Harappa itself built towns with walls of mud brick and made pottery on the wheel.</blockquote><a href="/culture/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Dama Dam Mast Qalandar&quot;, &quot;Every breath for the ecstasy of Qalandar&quot;, is a Sufi qawwali in honour of Lal Shahbaz Qalandar (1177–1274) of Sehwan Sharif, the most revered Sufi saint of <b>Sindh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dama-dam-mast-qalandar">Dama Dam Mast Qalandar</a></li></ul></section>
